2186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360563 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $w(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360563 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3f6701f-c993-5ff2-926d-7219617af2de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360563 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r} & =(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2r({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $u(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f89a4f6-9145-5fdf-84a8-9fcaed162c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360581 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $u(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂u}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂u}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z}=2{x}^{2}z+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7a6bf9a-03da-5b3f-8e6f-6e42220c784c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360583 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=8{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186368501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186368501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368496 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "77fe4fb6-8827-5540-a442-722c8b1aa5b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368497 #edb/ref 1080 "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368497 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368497 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "79830e35-fb58-5c0c-8ffe-3455034ae32b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368498 #edb/ref 1080 "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368498 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186368498 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e18a672